/**
 * CADDIE — full app v2
 * Screens: Welcome → Profile (per-club distances) → Courses → Planner (auto route)
 *          → Shot view (focused, with live Claude caddie advice + manual shot tracking)
 *          → Scorecard. Map toggle: OSM vector ↔ satellite imagery.
 * Data © OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL). Flyover: SKEYEVIEW (YouTube), deep-linked per hole.
 * Caddie advice: live Anthropic API call from the artifact runtime.
 */

// ============================================================================
// THE CADDIE SKILL — how elite caddies actually manage a course.
// Distilled from professional course-management methodology (Mark Broadie's
// strokes-gained work, Scott Fawcett's DECADE system, and tour-caddie practice).
// One source of truth: the math engine reads the weights; the LLM reads the prose.
// ============================================================================
const GOLF_RULES = {
  // Strokes-gained penalties added to expected strokes from each lie. Calibrated so
  // the fairway is strongly preferred and a lost-ball/OB result is punished hardest,
  // because a double bogey costs ~6x what a bogey does (Fawcett). Avoiding the big
  // number — not chasing birdies — is what lowers scores.
  liePenalty: { fairway: 0, green: -0.45, rough: 0.45, bunker: 0.75, trees: 1.15, ob: 2.4, water: 1.7 },
  // Explicit reward when a candidate most often finishes on short grass.
  fairwayBonus: 0.20,
  // The caddie's decision principles, shown verbatim to the model. Each is a real
  // tour-level course-management tenet, ordered by priority.
  text: [
    "GOAL IS FEWER BOGEYS, NOT MORE BIRDIES. A double bogey costs about six times what a bogey does. Every decision should first protect against the big number, then seek opportunity. Take what the hole gives.",
    "PLAY TO BUFFER ZONES, NOT 'AWAY FROM' TROUBLE. If water or OB runs down one side, the target is the centre (or far-centre) of the safe landing area — a specific spot that removes the hazard from the equation entirely — never just 'the other side'.",
    "ASSESS PERSONAL PROBABILITY HONESTLY. Judge a shot by THIS player's realistic success rate given their handicap and dispersion, not ideal execution. If a carry or a tight line isn't a high-percentage play for them, it's the wrong play, however tempting.",
    "ALWAYS PLAN THE SAFE MISS. Before committing, name where a slightly mishit shot finishes. Choose the line and club whose miss still leaves a reasonable next shot. A good miss is a skill, not luck.",
    "FAVOUR THE FAIRWAY; POSITION BEATS DISTANCE. A shorter shot from the fairway beats a longer one from rough, sand, or trees. On tight holes prefer the club that keeps the ball in play — but see the next rule before automatically clubbing down.",
    "TEE-SHOT MATH CAN FAVOUR MORE CLUB. Being 60-80 yards closer is often worth a little accuracy IF there is no lost-ball/OB penalty in the landing zone — a wider area further on can beat a tight lay-up that leaves a long approach. Only club down when the trouble is genuinely in reach of their dispersion.",
    "CENTRE OF THE GREEN ON APPROACH; NEVER SHORT-SIDE. Aim at the fat part of the green, not a tucked pin. You make more pars from 30 feet than from a short-sided bunker. Treat pins guarded by trouble as 'sucker pins' that demand a centre-green target.",
    "SHAPE THE BALL AWAY FROM DANGER. When suggesting a shot shape, the ball should curve away from the trouble so the miss is on the safe side; bias the aim to keep the player's typical miss safe.",
    "LAY UP TO A FULL, COMFORTABLE NUMBER. If laying up, leave a stock full-swing yardage the player likes, not an awkward in-between distance that forces a half shot.",
  ],
};
function rulesForPrompt() { return GOLF_RULES.text.map((r, i) => `${i + 1}. ${r}`).join("\n"); }


